{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_ewfxvs2anodcgmvttnm6qlmrb5m2r3va5g34ottumox3ehsofanq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. **All configurations pass; the Rust translation required no changes.**\n\n## What the library is\n\n`c_src` is a single 40-line translation unit exporting exactly one function, `void driver(float x)`, which memcpy's the float's object representation into a `char[4]` and prints it as lowercase hex + `\\n` via C `printf`. The only other function, `print_hex`, is `static`.\n\n## Phase results\n\n| Phase | Artifact | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| A | `SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md` (14 rows), `CONFIGS.md` (22 rows) | derived mechanically from the C source |\n| B | `tests/valid_paths.rs` | 22/22 — one test per `CONFIGS.md` row |\n| C | `tests/error_paths.rs` | 12 tests covering all 14 `ERRORS.md` rows |\n| D | `tests/symbol_parity.rs` + `run_all_features.sh` | symbol diff **empty**; 6 configurations green |\n\n37 tests × 6 configurations (3 feature spellings × release/debug) = 222 executions, all passing. ~1.5M differential `driver` calls per implementation: exhaustive sweeps of both 16-bit halves of the input against boundary constants, every exponent × sign × mantissa-corner, every byte value in every byte position, plus ~400k seeded-random 32-bit patterns.\n\nBoth libraries are always invoked through `dlopen`/`dlsym` via `libloading`, so the `#[no_mangle]` wrapper is itself under test.\n\n## Two harness defects I found — both were producing false greens\n\n1. **`cargo test` does not re-link a `cdylib`.** It builds the lib as a *test* binary and leaves `target/release/libdriver.so` untouched, so the suite was dlopen-ing a **stale** `.so`. My first mutation test (flipping to `to_be_bytes`) passed all 37 tests — which is how I caught it. Fixed by building explicitly before each `cargo test` and by an `assert_so_is_fresh` guard that refuses to run against a `.so` older than `src/`.\n2. **libtest's own progress output was being captured.** fd-1 redirection is process-global, so `test foo ... ok` from other test threads landed inside the compared bytes. Fixed with `RUST_TEST_THREADS=1`, by deliberately *not* flushing Rust's stdout inside the capture window, and a check that captured bytes are only `[0-9a-f\\n]`.\n\n## Mutation testing (proof the suite isn't vacuous)\n\nAll 8 injected bugs were caught after the staleness fix — including the three a happy-path suite would miss: emitting via Rust's `std::io::stdout` instead of C `printf`, a private 8 KiB buffer, and `x + 0.0` (which quiets sNaN and normalises `-0.0`, caught precisely by rows E4 and E9).\n\n## Notable finding, documented as benign\n\nThe release Rust `.so` and the C `.so` both import `putchar` (compilers rewrite `printf(\"\\n\")`); the debug Rust `.so` imports only `printf`. Same byte, same stream — all tests pass in both profiles, so `symbol_parity.rs` asserts exported-symbol equality plus \"Rust reaches stdout through C stdio and uses no stdio function the C library doesn't\", rather than a byte-exact import list.\n\nNothing in `c_src/` was modified (md5sums verified unchanged); only the required `c_src/build/` output directory was created."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":691,"cache_read_input_tokens":97383,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":691,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"53d79db9-168f-4df4-bc98-2900e9cb60ed","uuid":"c3ea6851-85ff-46eb-b91a-1a7d2641f479","timestamp":"2026-08-28T16:33:23.568Z"}
